<|start_of_role|>assistant<|end_of_role|>Here's a Python implementation of the quicksort algorithm:

```python
def quicksort(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    pivot = arr[len(arr) // 2]
    left = [x for x in arr if x < pivot]
    middle = [x for x in arr if x == pivot]
    right = [x for x in arr if x > pivot]
    return quicksort(left) + middle + quicksort(right)
```

The time complexity of quicksort is O(n log n) in the average and best cases, where n is the number of elements in the input array. This is because quicksort follows a divide-and-conquer strategy, recursively sorting the elements on either side of a chosen pivot. The average case occurs when the pivot divides the array into two nearly equal halves, leading to a balanced partition. In the best case, when the pivot is always the median, quicksort achieves this optimal time complexity.

However, the worst-case time complexity of quicksort is O(n^2), which happens when the input array is already sorted or nearly sorted, and the chosen pivot consistently divides the array



